{"evt":"summary_set","id":"note:note-mqq01krivx2","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","summary":"Multi-language answer for the native code onboarder (the Phase-1 @babel/parser extractor is JS/TS-only by design). Make extraction PLUGGABLE: a single interface extractFile(path, source) -> {symbols, edges} with per-language backends behind a registry keyed by file extension. JS/TS/JSX -> @babel/parser (done — richest JS/TS fidelity incl TS types + the CJS/ESM export handling, zero native deps). All OTHER languages -> tree-sitter, specifically web-tree-sitter (WASM grammars) NOT native tree-sitter — WASM needs no node-gyp/per-platform native build, so it is portable across Windows/mac/Linux and dodges the native-binary pain we hit with worktree node_modules; vendor the .wasm grammar files + write one symbol/call/import query (.scm) per language. The Phase-1 output shape ({name,kind,file,start/end line,signature,exported} + calls/imports edges) is already language-agnostic, so the Phase-2 code_node layer consumes any backend's output uniformly — adding a language NEVER touches the daemon, only adds a grammar + query. KEY FRAMING: tree-sitter is exactly the engine cmm + graphify use under the hood (158 langs) — so 'our way' for breadth is NOT reinventing parsing, it reuses the proven parser and owns only the index+retrieval layer, which is where the benchmark proved our KB beats cmm. Hybrid (keep @babel for JS/TS + tree-sitter for the rest) vs all-tree-sitter is a fidelity-vs-uniformity tradeoff the pluggable interface DEFERS — start hybrid, can migrate later. OPEN FORK (user decision): language BREADTH — JS/TS only (own stack, @babel suffices) / a common handful (Python, Go, Rust, Java, C/C++ via tree-sitter) / broad arbitrary (toward cmm's 158). Marginal cost per language is one extraction query (~50 lines), not new engine work — breadth is a dial, not a rebuild.","actor":"overlay-sync","ts":"2026-06-23T02:28:46.848Z"}
